<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Groningen</h1><p class="st-meta">1536 CE – 1594 CE · capital Groningen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The city of Groningen and its surrounding Ommelanden under Habsburg rule, a reluctant province that joined the Dutch Republic only after a siege in 1594.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, a Hanseatic city that dominated the Frisian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ommelanden">Ommelanden</a> around it, resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rule and placed itself under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders">Charles of Egmond</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a>, in 1515. When Guelders weakened, the city and countryside accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> as lord in 1536, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Stad en Lande, the city and the lands, was governed by its own estates in which the city and the rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ommelanden">Ommelanden</a> constantly quarrelled over staple rights and taxes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> ruled through a stadtholder. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> traded grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and kept close ties with the German <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanse</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1580 the stadtholder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-van-lalaing-count-of-rennenberg">Rennenberg</a> went over to the Spanish side, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> royalist. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> besieged and took the city in 1594, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> as a province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-groningen.jpg" alt="Lordship of Groningen" width="268" height="317"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Groningen_1559-1608.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1536 CE – 1594 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Groningen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles V, Philip II of Spain</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low Saxon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Stad en Lande, Groningen and Ommelanden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=lordship-of-groningen" aria-label="Lordship of Groningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4826 346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Groningen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5146 602L5102 664M5146 602L5099 670M5146 602L5097 634"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5146 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5146 602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Groningen. <a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=lordship-of-groningen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
